<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · disaster</p><h1 class="enc-title">Castle Bravo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1954</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Castle Bravo was the first American test of a deliverable dry-fuel hydrogen bomb, exploded at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bikini-atoll">Bikini Atoll</a> on 1 March 1954. Its yield of about 15 megatons, far more than predicted, made it the largest nuclear explosion ever carried out by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. Radioactive fallout contaminated the inhabited atolls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rongelap">Rongelap</a> and Utirik and the Japanese fishing boat Daigo Fukuryū Maru, causing an international outcry.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/castle-bravo.jpg" alt="Castle Bravo" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: USDE · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CastleBravo1.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
